 ## Subtopics
 
-### Grid Deformation with Entropy Coding
-Compresses data differences between registered adjacent data slices using entropy coding [S3, unofficial]. This variant focuses on storage efficiency rather than just structural merging, often applied after merging or as part of a compression pipeline for large-scale volume data [S3, unofficial].
+### [Multiply Compositing](8122-multiply-compositing.md)
+Merges two sparse hash grids by taking the union of their active voxels, where overlapping values are multiplied together. It differs from [Replace Compositing](7905-multi-grid-merging.md) by preserving both contributions in the overlap rather than letting one overwrite the other, making it suitable for additive or multiplicative blending of volumetric data.
 
 ### Max Compositing
-Returns a new grid whose active voxels are the union of two grids, taking the maximum value at overlapping coordinates [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Replace Compositing](7905-multi-grid-merging.md) by preserving the highest contribution in the overlap, making it suitable for scenarios where the strongest signal (e.g., highest density or opacity) is desired.
+Returns a new grid whose active voxels are the union of two grids, taking the maximum value at overlapping coordinates [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Replace Compositing](7905-multi-grid-merging.md) by preserving the highest contribution in the overlap rather than letting one overwrite the other, making it suitable for scenarios where the strongest signal is desired.
 
 ### Sum Compositing
-Returns a new grid whose active voxels are the union of two grids, summing overlapping values [S1, unofficial]. This is suitable for additive blending of volumetric data, such as combining density fields from multiple sources.
+Returns a new grid whose active voxels are the union of two grids, summing overlapping values [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Replace Compositing](7905-multi-grid-merging.md) by preserving the combined contribution in the overlap rather than letting one overwrite the other, making it suitable for additive blending of volumetric data.
